        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "374255",
          "text": "<p>Let $\\text{R}$ be a relation from a set $\\text{A}$ to a set $\\text{B}.$ The inverse relation from $\\text{B}$ to $\\text{A},$ denoted by $\\text{R}^{-1},$&nbsp;is the set of ordered pairs $\\{(b,a) \\mid (a,b) \\in \\text{R}\\}$.</p><ul><li>$\\text{S1: R}$ is reflexive relation iff $\\text{R}^{-1} = \\text{R}$</li><li>$\\text{S2: R}$ is a symmetric relation iff $\\text{R}^{-1} = \\text{R}$</li></ul><p>Which one of the following statements is true?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>Only $\\text{S1}$</li><li>Only $\\text{S2}$</li><li>Both $\\text{S1}$ and $\\text{S2}$</li><li>None of the above</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_dm_tw_2,goclasses-testseries,goclasses,set-theory&algebra,relations,easy,two-marks",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "374276",
          "text": "Given a set of values $\\text{R} = \\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7\\}.$ The number of relations on this set which are both partial-order and equivalence relation is?",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "1",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_dm_tw_2,goclasses-testseries,goclasses,set-theory&algebra,relations,partial-order,numerical-answers,moderate,two-marks",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "374277",
          "text": "<p>For any set $\\text{X},$ let $|\\text{X}|$ be the cardinality of set $\\text{X}.$ Let $\\text{A}$ and $\\text{B}$ be two finite sets such that $|\\text{A}| = |\\text{B}|$. There is a one to one function $f$ from $\\text{A}$ to $\\text{B}.$</p><p>Which of the following must be true for $f?$</p><ul><li>$\\text{S1:}\\; f$ is onto function</li><li>$\\text{S2:}\\;f$ has an inverse</li></ul><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>Only $\\text{S1}$</li><li>Only $\\text{S2}$</li><li>Both $\\text{S1}$ and $\\text{S2}$</li><li>None of the above</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_dm_tw_2,goclasses-testseries,goclasses,set-theory&algebra,functions,moderate,two-marks",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "466669",
          "text": "<p>Let $<br>R_{1} \\text { and } R_{2} \\text { be relations on a set } A \\text {. }<br>$</p><p>Consider the following statements:</p><p>M: if $R_{1}$ and $R_{2}$ are both reflexive, then so is $R_{1} \\cup R_{2}$.</p><p>N: if $R_{1}$ and $R_{2}$ are both symmetric, then so is $R_{1} \\cup R_{2}$.</p><p>Which of the above statements is/are true?</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>Only M&nbsp;</li><li>Only N&nbsp;</li><li>Both&nbsp;</li><li>None</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "C",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses,goclasses_cs_dm_tw_2,goclasses-testseries,set-theory&algebra,two-marks",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "374264",
          "text": "<p>A binary relation $\\text{R}$ on a set $\\text{A}$ is called connected iff for all elements $x$ and $y$ of $\\text{A},$ either $x \\text{R} y$ or $y \\text{R} x$ or both. i.e. $\\text{R}$ is connected iff $\\forall x,y \\in \\text{A} ((x \\text{R} y)$ $\\vee$&nbsp;&nbsp;$(y \\text{R} x))$</p><p>Which of the following is/are true ?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>Every connected relation is reflexive.</li><li>Every connected relation is partial order.</li><li>Every connected relation is symmetric.&nbsp;&nbsp;</li><li>Every connected relation is transitive.</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_dm_tw_2,goclasses-testseries,goclasses,set-theory&algebra,relations,partial-order,multiple-selects,moderate,two-marks",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "466665",
          "text": "<p>$\\{2,4\\}$ is an element of the following sets:</p><p> </p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>&nbsp;$\\{\\{\\{2,4\\}\\}\\}$</li><li>&nbsp;$\\{1,2,3,4,\\{2,4\\}\\}$</li><li>&nbsp;$\\{\\{1\\},\\{4\\},\\{2,4\\}\\}$</li><li>&nbsp;$\\left\\{\\left(x, x^{2}\\right) \\mid x \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\right\\}$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "B;C",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses,goclasses_cs_dm_tw_2,goclasses-testseries,set-theory&algebra,multiple-selects,one-mark",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "403069",
          "text": "<p>Let $f$ be a function from a set $X$ to a set $Y$. Consider the following statements.</p><ul><li>$P$ : For each $x \\in X$, there exists $y \\in Y$ such that $f(x)=y$.</li><li>$Q$ : For each $y \\in Y$, there exists $x \\in X$ such that $f(x)=y$.</li><li>$R$ : There exist $x_1, x_2 \\in X$ such that $x_1 \\neq x_2$ and $f\\left(x_1\\right)=f\\left(x_2\\right)$.</li></ul><p>The negation of the statement \" $f$ is one-to-one and onto $Y$ \" is</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>$P$ or not $R$</li><li>$R$ or not $P$</li><li>$R$ or not $Q$</li><li>$P$ and not $R$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_dm_tw_2,goclasses-testseries,goclasses,set-theory&algebra,functions,moderate,two-marks",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "403072",
          "text": "Let $A=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}$ and $B=\\{2,3,4\\}$. How many sets $C$ have the property that $C \\subseteq A$ and $B \\subseteq C?$",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "4",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_dm_tw_2,goclasses-testseries,numerical-answers,goclasses,set-theory&algebra,set-theory,easy,one-mark",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "466667",
          "text": "<p>For each of the given (input, output) relationships in Tables One and Two; answer the following:</p><p><img alt=\"\" width=\"349\" height=\"211\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></p><p>Which of the following is true?</p><ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type: upper-alpha;\"><li>A a function of $B$</li><li>B a function of $A$</li><li>C a function of D</li><li>D a function of C</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "B;C;D",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses,goclasses_cs_dm_tw_2,goclasses-testseries,set-theory&algebra,multiple-selects,one-mark",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "374284",
          "text": "Let $\\text{A, B}$ be two non-empty sets, with cardinality $3,4$ respectively. Let $\\text{R}$ be a relation defined on the power set of $\\text{A} \\times \\text{B}.$ Relation $\\text{R}$ is reflexive, symmetric, transitive, and antisymmetric. Hence,  Relation $ \\text{R}$ is also equivalence relation.\n\nThe cardinality of the largest equivalence class of relation $\\text{R}$ is?",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "1",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_dm_tw_2,goclasses-testseries,numerical-answers,goclasses,set-theory&algebra,relations,equivalence-class,moderate,two-marks",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "403073",
          "text": "<p>Let $A$ and $B$ be arbitrary sets and consider the set $S$ defined below:<br>$$<br>S=\\{x \\mid \\neg(x \\in A \\rightarrow x \\in B)\\}<br>$$<br>What is the correct expression for $S$ in terms of $A$ and $B$ using the standard set operators?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>$A \\cup B$</li><li>$A \\cap B$</li><li>$A \\backslash B$</li><li>$B \\backslash A$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_dm_tw_2,goclasses-testseries,goclasses,set-theory&algebra,set-theory,easy,one-mark",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "403068",
          "text": "<p>Let's suppose that we have a function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$. We'll say that $f$ is an odd function if the following is true:<br>$$<br>\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R} . f(-x)=-f(x)<br>$$<br>We can define even functions as follows. A function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ is called even if the following is true:<br>$$<br>\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R} . f(-x)=f(x)<br>$$<br>Which of the following statements is/are correct?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>If $f: \\mathbb{R} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ and $g: \\mathbb{R} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ are odd, then $g \\circ f$ is also odd.</li><li>If $f: \\mathbb{R} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ is odd and is a bijection, then $f^{-1}$ is also odd.</li><li>If $f: \\mathbb{R} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ is an even function, then $f$ is not a bijection.</li><li>Let $f: \\mathbb{R} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ be any function. Then $g: \\mathbb{R} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined as $g(x)=f(x)-f(-x)$ is odd.</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "a;b;c;d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_dm_tw_2,goclasses-testseries,goclasses,set-theory&algebra,functions,multiple-selects,difficult,two-marks",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "403071",
          "text": "<p>Suppose that $S$ and $T$ are sets. Which of the following first-order logic statements are translations of the statement \" $S$ is not a subset of $T$ ?\" Check all that apply.</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>$\\forall x .(x \\in S \\rightarrow x \\notin T)$</li><li>$\\forall x .(x \\in S \\wedge x \\notin T)$</li><li>$\\exists x .(x \\in S \\rightarrow x \\notin T)$</li><li>$\\exists x .(x \\in S \\wedge x \\notin T)$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_dm_tw_2,goclasses-testseries,goclasses,set-theory&algebra,set-theory,multiple-selects,easy,one-mark",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "466671",
          "text": "Let $\\mathrm{A}=\\{\\mathrm{a}, \\mathrm{b}, \\mathrm{c}\\}$. How many different binary relations are there over $\\mathrm{A}$ that are partial orders?",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "19",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses,goclasses_cs_dm_tw_2,goclasses-testseries,set-theory&algebra,numerical-answers,two-marks",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "374290",
          "text": "<p>Let $\\text{R}$ be an equivalence relation on a non-empty set $\\text{A}.$ Let $a,b \\in \\text{A}.$</p><p>For any $x \\in A,$ let $[x]$ denote the equivalence class of $\\text{R}$ containing $x.$</p><p>Which of the following is/are correct statements?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>$a\\text{R}b$ iff $[a] = [b]$</li><li>$a\\text{R}b$ iff $[a] \\cap&nbsp;[b] \\neq&nbsp;\\phi$</li><li>If $\\text{A}$ is non-empty finite set then $\\text{R}$ can never have more than $|\\text{A}|$ equivalence classes, where $|\\text{A}|$ is the cardinality of $\\text{A}.$</li><li>$\\text{R} = \\text{A} \\times \\text{A}$ iff $R$ has only one equivalence class.</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "a;b;c;d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_dm_tw_2,goclasses-testseries,goclasses,set-theory&algebra,relations,equivalence-class,multiple-selects,moderate,two-marks",
          "category": "Set Theory & Algebra"
        }
      ]
    }
